{"answer":"The community pot is a shared basket of fish that we put back into the lake when we exceed 10 kg.  The fisherman who takes the excess is expected to hand the extra fish over to the log‑keeper at the dock.  The log‑keeper keeps a small box in the community hall, where all donated fish are stored in a cool, covered area.  When the community gathers for the monthly feast or when the children need a protein‑rich meal, the pot is divided fairly among those who have no personal catch or who need food for family.  If any fisherman has an excess, we donate it to the pot so everyone can benefit.

The pot is not used for selling; it is only for communal use – feasts, children’s meals, or to help those who have fallen behind.
","revised_policy":"No individual may take more than 10 kg from the lake per fishing trip.  At the start of each trip, each fisher sets a personal catch limit of 10 kg.  We weigh our haul, and if it exceeds 10 kg, we must release the extra fish back into the lake or donate them to the community pot for communal use.  The community will keep a log of catches, and if someone repeatedly (defined as two separate incidents) exceeds the limit, they lose fishing rights for the next day.  All fish taken below the limit are kept by the individual for personal consumption or sale.  Each fisher weighs his own haul at the dock before leaving the lake.  After the catch, we all gather at the communal weigh‑station and confirm the weight.  The appointed log keeper records every fisher’s weight and notes any excess.  At the end of the day the community elders meet; based on the log, they decide if any fisher has repeated violations (two incidents) and revoke his fishing rights for the next day.","revised_operationalization":"The donated fish are collected by the log keeper at the dock, placed in a communal box in the community hall, stored in a cool, covered area, and later used for communal feasts or children’s meals.  No changes to the rest of the operationalization.","reasoning":"I wanted to explain how the pot works, who handles it, where it sits, and its purpose so that the community understands that donated fish are kept for communal benefit and not sold."}
